.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKV 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekv \- store and read items in a key/value table
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B ekv
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B ekv
module is euclid's key/value store: tables of items, each identified by
a
.B partition key
and optionally ordered within that partition by a
.BR "sort key" .
.PP
It is for the data that has no business being an object in a bucket \-
a record read one at a time by name and updated in place, rather than a
file written once and found again by listing a prefix. A store of
supplier records, of per-tenant settings, of the last state of
something: things that are looked up, not streamed.
.SH ITEMS ARE JSON
An item is an ordinary JSON object. It nests as deeply as you like,
there are no type annotations to write and none to read back, and the
types survive the round trip: a number written as a number is read as a
number,
.I 3
does not come back as
.IR 3.0 ,
an empty object stays an empty object, and a list keeps its order.
.PP
Attribute names may not be empty, start with
.I $
or contain
.IR . " \-"
those are refused when the item is written rather than quietly escaped,
so that what is read back is exactly what was written.
.PP
Every item that is read carries
.I _created
and
.IR _modified ,
added by the server. An attribute of either name would be shadowed by
them.
.SH KEYS
A table declares what its items are keyed on when it is created, and
what type that key has \-
.IR string ,
.I number
or
.IR binary .
Every item written has to carry that attribute, with that type; one
that does not is refused rather than stored where no lookup could find
it.
.PP
The type is not decoration. A
.I number
sort key orders 2, 9, 10, 100; the same values under a
.I string
key order 10 before 9, because that is what those characters do. Choose
the type the values actually have.
.PP
With no sort key, an item is identified by its partition key alone and
can only be read one at a time. With one, the items sharing a partition
key are ordered by it, and
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-query (1)
can read any range of them \- which is the only reason to declare one.
.SH WRITES REPLACE
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-put-item (1)
writes the item you give it, whole. It does not merge with what was
there: writing
.I {"supplierId":"4711","name":"x"}
over a record of twenty attributes leaves a record of two.
.PP
There is no partial update yet. Until there is, a partial change is a
read, an edit and a write, and two of those racing will lose one of the
edits.
.SH NAMESPACES
A table belongs to the account you are logged in to and to the
namespace the session is scoped to \- see
.BR euclid-cli-eam-change-namespace (1).
Two environments each having a
.I suppliers
table is the ordinary case rather than a collision.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B create-table
Creates a table and says what its items are keyed on. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-create-table (1).
.TP
.B describe-table
Shows a table's key and how many items it holds. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-describe-table (1).
.TP
.B list-tables
Lists the account's tables. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-list-tables (1).
.TP
.B delete-table
Deletes a table and everything in it. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-delete-table (1).
.TP
.B put-item
Writes an item, replacing whatever was under its key. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-put-item (1).
.TP
.B get-item
Reads one item by its key. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-get-item (1).
.TP
.B delete-item
Removes one item by its key. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-delete-item (1).
.TP
.B query
Reads one partition's items, in sort-key order. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-query (1).
.TP
.B scan
Reads a table's items, a page at a time. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-scan (1).
.SH VALUES ON THE COMMAND LINE
Anywhere a key or a bound is given, it is given as JSON \- so a string
is quoted and a number is not:
.PP
.RS
.nf
--partition-key '"4711"'     # a string key
--partition-key 4711         # a number key
.fi
.RE
.PP
The shell's single quotes keep the JSON quotes from being eaten. Items
and keys can be read from a file instead, with
.B \-\-item\-file
and
.BR \-\-key\-file ,
which is what to use for anything you would not want to quote twice.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are given to
.B euclid-cli
itself, before the
.I ekv
module name.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
Euclid gateway endpoint to talk to, e.g. \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system trust
store, e.g. for self-signed development certificates.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print top-level usage and exit.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.B ekv
commands are authenticated using the session created by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
loaded from
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials ,
and signed with RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures using the stored
access key if there is one.
.SH LIMITS
What is not there yet, in the order it is likely to arrive: partial
updates and conditional writes, batch reads and writes, secondary
indexes, a time-to-live attribute, and item changes published on the
event bus.
.PP
Paging is by page size and index, as everywhere else in euclid, rather
than by a cursor. A page taken from a table that is being written to at
the same time can repeat an item or miss one.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The action completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Argument parsing failed, the request could not be sent, or the server
rejected it. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
A store of supplier records, and a log of what each of them delivered:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ekv create-table --name suppliers --partition-key supplierId
euclid-cli ekv put-item --table suppliers --item-file supplier.json
euclid-cli ekv get-item --table suppliers --key '{"supplierId":"4711"}'

euclid-cli ekv create-table --name deliveries \e
    --partition-key supplierId --sort-key deliveredAt

euclid-cli ekv query --table deliveries --partition-key '"4711"' \e
    --operator begins-with --value '"2026-09"'
.fi
.RE
